The Korean War is one of the most complex and multifaceted conflicts of the 20th century, with both sides having their own set of motivations and goals. While the North Korean government and its allies claim that they were fighting against the aggressive policies of the United States and South Korea, many South Korean experts argue that the war was solely initiated by South Korea and the North's involvement was purely defensive.

According to these experts, the South Korean government led by Syngman Rhee was determined to unify the entire Korean peninsula under its rule and saw the North as a major obstacle to achieving this goal. Therefore, they launched a surprise attack on the North on June 25, 1950, hoping to quickly defeat the North Korean army and unify the peninsula under its control.

However, the North Korean army was better prepared and better equipped than expected, and they were able to push the South Korean army back across the 38th parallel. At this point, the United States intervened on behalf of South Korea, and the war escalated into a full-scale conflict involving several countries from both sides.

